<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lot-et-Garonne</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lot-et-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the Nouvelle-Aquitaine region, named after the Lot and Garonne rivers, which meet within it, with its prefecture at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agen">Agen</a>. Created in 1790 from parts of the old provinces of Guyenne and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, it is an agricultural area known for the prunes of Agen. In antiquity its south was the land of the Aquitanian Sotiates.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lot-et-garonne.jpg" alt="Lot-et-Garonne" width="320" height="169" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MOSSOT · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agen_-_Pr%C3%A9fecture,_ancien_palais_%C3%A9piscopal_-2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
